 to be where nighthorse and  henry are being held captive henry  however manages to get the upper hand on  one of his captives and escape just in  time to run into the arriving walt they  all work together to systematically take  down matthias's men malachi then hangs  night horse from a chain and forces the  cowboy sheriff to choose between chasing  him and saving someone he despises after  some deliberation walt decides to save  nighthorse walt spent six seasons  accusing nighthorse of the most heinous  crimes while nighthorse is not remotely  perfect walt's saving him is a huge  moment hey  [Music]  thank you  throughout the various action-packed  events that take place in goodbye as  always implied there are also several  side plots occurring the biggest one  arguably is the blossoming romance  between vic and walt the two finally  recognize their feelings for each other  toward the end of the show while it's a  moving moment and a natural conclusion  to their intertwined stories it does not  quite end there vic confesses that the  two will never be the priority in each  other's lives because they are  ultimately devoted to someone else walt  to his deceased wife and vic to the  unborn child that she lost in season 5  of longmire vic also confesses her fear  of losing walt this is a theme that the  show has explored since the beginning  loss and grief may feel like the end but  in reality one lives on anyway it's a  poignant conclusion to what is arguably  one of the big mission statements for  the entire show  i think that's kind of the point of  everything  after the team stops malachi  nighthorse's original plan comes to  fruition and henry takes over running  the casino in hopes of steering it in a  more honorable direction that will  benefit the tribe  earlier in goodbye as always implied  walt's admission that henry should take  over the casino despite how walt may  feel about the place seems to force  henry to think long and hard about the  decision he wanted me to run the casino  you should  what  henry has struggled with his place in  the tribe since the beginning of the  show and running the casino is probably  the place where he can affect the most  change for the cheyenne people unlike  nighthorse henry won't just assume  what's best for everyone and he also has  the respect that nighthorse ultimately  did not have throughout longmire it's  been a big assumption among the  characters that walt refuses to give up  being sheriff because he loves the job  too much however this assumption is  turned on its head in goodbye is always  implied when walt decides to pay his  daughter katie one last visit before the  show comes to an end walt asks her why  she thinks he never gave up being  sheriff and like most characters she  assumes that he loves his job too much  walt refutes this however saying that  he's simply been waiting for the right  person to replace him and he's finally  found them  the only person who can replace me  is you  this isn't a mission that sort of comes  out of left field and katie does seem  confused at first walt however says she  knows more about police methods than he  did when he joined the force katie  ultimately does decide to run for  sheriff after walt retires it's not  really something that feels natural to  katie's character but it's still easy to  believe a story of a daughter following  in her father's footsteps  the series comes to its conclusion with  walt retired and happy possibly  searching for the buried treasure that  lucian claimed to have found before his  death it also shows walt's growth as a  character as he now owns a cell phone a  piece of technology that he never owned  before it's a quiet moment and the smile  on walt's face illustrates that he is  now at peace with his life and the  things he has done some may argue that  it leaves the door open for more  longmire either with walt somehow drawn  back into another case or told through  the perspective of katie as the 